;; THE HUMAN GATES OF A STAGE, AS A RECORD - a file, not a status line in prose, so that
;; `bb next` can read where the stage stands the way the loop reads a merge decision. Copy to
;; `stage-N/stage-N-gates.edn` under `docs/stages/`, the stage's folder, when the stage is pulled. Each gate is nil until
;; the person passes it, then a map with the time and, for a sign-off, who signed: `:by` is a
;; name, never a role ("the person"). The time is `:at`, ISO-8601 with its offset - `bb next`
;; gives one to paste, in the project's zone; a record written before `:at` carries `:date`. Nothing but the person writes one, and a gate once passed
;; is not unpassed - a stage that loops back re-runs its exit criteria and closes again with a
;; later date. Three gates for every stage, and a fourth for a pre-release stage. "The exit
;; criteria section" below is the stage plan's section headed "Exit criteria (the stage's gate), and
;; the cap", whose number differs by the stage's kind. A comment carries no mark to fill.
{:stage/id "<N>"
 ;; the stage plan approved, with its cap (the exit criteria section): {:at "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM+HH:MM" :cap "$n" :by "NAME OF WHO SIGNED"}
 :stage/approved nil
 ;; the blueprint signed off (workflow step 10): {:at "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM+HH:MM" :by "NAME OF WHO SIGNED"}
 :blueprint/signed nil
 ;; A PRE-RELEASE STAGE ONLY: the threat model signed - every line of 02-architecture.md §15
 ;; answered, each with its evidence (the stage plan's §7): {:at "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM+HH:MM" :by "NAME OF WHO SIGNED"}.
 ;; Another kind leaves it nil; `bb next` asks for it of a pre-release stage, before it closes
 :security/signed nil
 ;; A STAGE BEFORE PRE-RELEASE ONLY, when the person skips the security reviewer panel at its end -
 ;; the session recommending from what the stage changed: {:at "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM+HH:MM" :by "NAME OF WHO DECIDED"
 ;; :reason "why, in a sentence"}. A pre-release stage never skips it; `bb next` says so if one does
 :security/skipped nil
 ;; the exit criteria met, deployed locally and walked (the exit criteria section): {:at "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM+HH:MM" :mvp? false}
 :stage/closed nil
 ;; A STAGE AMENDED AFTER ITS PACKETS RAN, one pass at a time (method §04): each pass an entry
 ;; {:q "Q-n" :opened "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM+HH:MM" :approved {:at :cap :by} :signed {:at :by}}. `bb stage-amend Q-n`
 ;; writes :q and :opened - a KIT command, not a hand - and the two sign-offs inside are the
 ;; person's, as the gates above are. The first approval and sign-off stay as they were; the
 ;; latest re-approval's :cap is the stage's cap. `bb next` asks for each gate again in turn. A
 ;; pre-release stage's threat model is signed once, at the close, after the last amendment: a
 ;; signing from before an amendment's packets merged stays, and the new one goes in the last pass
 ;; as :security/signed {:at :by} - never over the first; the latest by date stands
 :stage/amendments nil}
